module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.bare_reference_channel_declines_a_pull_in_silence

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data bare_reference_channel_declines_a_pull_in_silence: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "bare_reference_channel_declines_a_pull_in_silence" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "bare reference channel declines a pull in silence: the loader's bare-reference channel decides, per NAME, whether to pull the module that declares it. Every arm that declines is a bare `continue` -- no diagnostic, no count, no record carried past the loader -- so a module whose type reference was declined is simply not compile-clean. It then drops out of the compiled closure into the NAME CENSUS ONLY, the item registry is built over REALIZED modules only, and the first LOUD symptom is `ResolvedCalleeRegistryRowAbsent` (`effect summary incomplete`) raised once per CALLING witness, modules away from the file that carries the defect. DESIGN section 5's absorbing fallback: a precise, located fact (this reference names a module nothing pulled) replaced by a wider, quieter state, with the locating signal destroyed.",

    "THE RULE, DERIVED FROM THE LOADER AND NAMED AT ITS OWNING SYMBOLS. There are TWO gates, in this order. GATE ONE, `v1_compiler.cli_run` `build_both_closure_edge_index`: the bare half runs only when `source_declares_import_lines(&source.content)` is FALSE. A single `import` line anywhere in a file turns the ENTIRE bare channel off for that file, for every name in it. GATE TWO, `v1_compiler.cli_run` `visit_bare_reference_providers`: inside the channel a name is pulled only if the census answers `GlobalBareUniqueBinding` (or `closure_bare_disposition` answers `UniqueOnChain`) AND its local `pullable` predicate holds -- the reference is in call position, OR the declaration has params, OR it carries a `type_annotation`, OR its `connective` is not `NoConnective`. Names that are self-declared, explicitly imported, substrate vocabulary (`std_types::kernel_type_set` / `container_type_arity`) or a `test fn`/`test data` row are skipped earlier. `AmbiguousOnChain` is the ONE arm that refuses loudly; every other decline is silent.",

    "WHAT THAT MEANS FOR A NULLARY TYPE ALIAS, which is the shape that bit. the branded alias `type ProcessNodeId = NonEmptyStr where brand(...)` has no params, no type_annotation and `NoConnective`, so referencing it in TYPE POSITION satisfies no arm of `pullable` and its module is not pulled. A coproduct (`Disj`) or a record type IS pulled from the same position, because its connective is not `NoConnective`.",

    "THE STANDING FACT ABOUT IMPORT LISTS IS HALF TRUE AND THE HALF THAT IS FALSE IS THE DANGEROUS ONE. A `.dag` import list does NOT bind at NAME RESOLUTION -- a name absent from the braces still resolves through the corpus-wide bare census (`v1_compiler.infer_env` `global_bare_fallback_invariant`), which is why an unlisted use is an ADVISORY and not an error. It DOES bind at the LOADER: gate one means the first `import` line an author adds silently switches off bare pulling for every OTHER name in that file. So a partially-imported file is strictly more dangerous than a zero-import one, and 'it has an import header' is not a safety property.",

    "THE DISCRIMINATING SET, hermetic: FOUR ENTRIES over seven modules in one source root, at `fixtures/bare_reference_channel/`. One compile each, same invocation, only the entry varying: `gunbc compile --output-dir <tmp> --source-root fixtures/bare_reference_channel --entry fixtures/bare_reference_channel/<name>.dag --target rust`. (1) `bare_record_consumer` -- no imports, bare reference to a RECORD type -- PULLS: the compile is clean and the reference is reported only as an `unlisted import use` advisory. (2) `bare_alias_consumer` -- no imports, bare reference to `type BrcAliasId = Int` -- DOES NOT PULL: the compile REFUSES with `unresolved type BrcAliasId` and the alias home is absent from the resolved closure. The pair differs in exactly the `pullable` condition. (3) `imported_record_consumer` -- the SAME bare reference as (1) plus one unrelated `import` line -- DOES NOT PULL: refuses with `unresolved type BrcRecordId`. The pair (1)/(3) differs in exactly gate one. (4) `transitive_alias_consumer` -- no imports, a bare CALL into a module that imports the alias home -- pulls the CALLEE, and the alias arrives as a passenger of its import closure, so the same alias reference that refuses in (2) compiles clean here. With `GUNBC_BARE_PULL_TRACE=1` a pulled name prints one `[bare-pull]` line naming the census state and a declined name prints nothing, which is the silence this row is about.",

    "THE HYPOTHESIS THIS ROW REPLACES, AND WHY IT LOOKED FALSIFIED. gunbc#11940 proposed that alias-shaped references are not pullable and then falsified it on `test.claim.extdeps_version_base`, which reaches `extdeps.version` through the same alias shape and compiles clean. The alias half was RIGHT; the counterexample resolves for a different reason. That witness also bare-references `min_coreutils_version`, a `data` declaration WITH a type annotation, so `pullable` holds, `extdeps.tools.gnu_coreutils` is pulled, and `extdeps.version` arrives as a PASSENGER of that module's import closure. Reproduced hermetically by entry (4) `transitive_alias_consumer`: a bare CALL to a function in a module that imports the alias home resolves the alias, while (2) identical alias reference still refuses. Whether a bare reference resolves is therefore not a property of the reference; it is a property of what ELSE the file happened to name.",

    "THE SET IS NOW A CONSUMED ONE, AND THE CONSUMER IS NOT THE RUNG (gunbc#11950). The four entries are executed by `gunbc test //gunbc/instruments:bare-reference-channel-outcome` (`gunbc.instrument_targets` `bare_reference_channel_expectations` / `bare_reference_channel_holds`, bound through `gunbc.target_binding` `BareReferenceChannelOutcomeProducer`). It asserts, per entry, the OUTCOME both gates jointly decide -- did the bare half RUN for this file, and which modules did it PULL -- at identity grain and never as a count, so a gate that moves flips exactly the row that isolates it. Each gate was perturbed in the seed and each flipped exactly its own entry and no other. THE INSTRUMENT EXISTS, IT IS HAND-INVOCABLE, AND THAT IS NOT A RUNG: it is on no required lane, instrument targets have no route into `//:required` by construction, and the CI job roster is closed to growth by operator ruling, so a gate that moves turns this target red only for whoever runs it and BLOCKS NOTHING. A reader who finds this row should therefore not re-file the instrument as unbuilt work -- it is built, it is enrolled in the corpus, and what is missing is a blocking acceptance path, which is the rung-1 trigger below and not another instrument. Its `.dag` classifier is exercised by `test.claim.target_invocation_witness`, whose bare-channel claims carry one red per gate, the join at identity grain, and subject-unreached separated from a moved gate; the roster is read there rather than counted here, because a transcribed count rots the first time a claim is added (DESIGN section 6).",

    "AN AMENDMENT TO THIS ROW'S OWN FRONTIER RECEIPT, STANDING INDEPENDENT OF ANY RUNG LINE. When gunbc#11943 recorded the four entries it named the instrument's trigger as the loader-side decline carrier -- the same carrier the CEILING trigger below names -- on the reasoning that building the instrument before the carrier existed would wall the SYMPTOM and not the decision. That sentence was STRONGER THAN THE OUTCOME-GRAINED WALL NEEDS, and gunbc#11950 is the demonstration: whether the bare half RAN for a file and which modules it PULLED are already values on `v1_compiler.cli_run` `BothClosureEdgeIndex`, so the decision's OUTCOME is observable today without any carrier. What the carrier is still required for is the REASON -- which arm declined a given name -- and that is the ceiling's subject, not this instrument's. The two grains were conflated in the earlier receipt and are separated here.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable, and only for the eventual symptom. The unresolved type is reported where it sits, so the file's own compile is loud; what is silent is the DECLINE (no arm of the loader records it) and the DEMOTION (`[census] N indexed modules outside the compile-clean closure enter the name census only` is a COUNT with no identities and no causes). Under a floor run that count is the whole record, and the located reading arrives 13 modules away wearing an effect-modelling error's name. THE RUNG STAYED AT 1 THROUGH gunbc#11950, WHICH BUILT THE DISCRIMINATING INSTRUMENT, and the reason is DESIGN section 4b's own definition: rung 2 is a mechanism that EXPOSES AND BLOCKS, and meta-obligation 1 requires a discriminating RED refused on the REAL ACCEPTANCE PATH. This instrument exposes and blocks nothing, so reporting 2 would be the inflation meta-obligation 1 names -- and an inflated class never ranks for climbing, which is the harm. Operator ruling 2026-09-21, on the escalation this row's own change raised: honest and cheap beats true-looking; a lane is a standing claim on a paid runner on every pull request and every queued landing, the roster is closed to growth, and that is not spent to make a rung sentence true.",

    "RUNG-1 TRIGGER, AT CAPABILITY GRAIN AND DELIBERATELY NOT AT ARTIFACT GRAIN: rung 2 is reached when a behaviour change in EITHER gate BLOCKS A LANDING -- that is, when the outcome discrimination over these four entries executes on the required acceptance path and its red refuses the merge. A trigger reading `enrol the instrument` would be ARTIFACT grain and is satisfiable by enrolling something that does not block, which is exactly the grain mismatch DESIGN section 4b(3) warns about; the capability is a blocking acceptance path, not a roster row. If this class later earns a lane it will be on its own merits, argued at `gunbc.witness_floor_workflow` `witness_floor_lane_jobs`, and not as a side effect of this row. DESIGN section 4b(4) binds meanwhile: the fixtures, the instrument and its claims STAY ENROLLED at rung 1, because they are what makes the eventual climb checkable and evidence does not retire on a climb.",

    "ATTAINABLE CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed, and it is NOT reachable in the change that authored this row. The condition is decidable -- at the decline the loader holds the file, the name, the declaring module and the reason -- but nothing carries a declined name past the loader, so the resolver that later reports `unresolved type 'X'` cannot say declared by module M, which this file bare channel declined, with the arm named, and the registry-row consumer cannot say callee module M is census-only. The missing thing is a CARRIER, not a check.",

    "CEILING TRIGGER (the climb to rung 3), at capability grain, and NOT the same trigger as the rung-1 one above -- that one is a blocking acceptance path over the OUTCOME, this one is the carrier that makes the DECISION unwritable, and satisfying either leaves the other standing: a per-file record of every bare-channel decline (name, declining arm, declaring module when the census named one) produced by the loader and CONSUMED by the two downstream reporters -- the unresolved-name diagnostic and the callee-registry join -- sufficient for each to name the unpulled module at the site that failed. A trigger naming only the loader-side record would be satisfied while both distant symptoms stayed unlocated, which is the grain mismatch DESIGN section 4b(3) warns about.",

    "THE DEEPER READING, kept separate because it is a proposal and not a measurement: `pullable` is a HEURISTIC standing where a decidable fact was available. The kind of the declaration the census resolved to is known exactly; the predicate instead asks four proxy questions about the node's shape and declines on all-no. DESIGN section 4 rules that in a closed system a heuristic is never necessary, and section 5 names the confidence threshold that selects such an arm as the tell that LOCATES anemic modelling. The principled repair is to pull the declarer of any bare name the census resolved, whatever its shape, and to price the resulting closure growth rather than guess at it -- which is a measured change to closure size across the corpus, not an edit that can ride in on this row.",

    "REVIEW TELL: a module with NO import header at all is not tidy, it is on the bare channel; a module with ONE import line has the channel off and is relying on that list being complete. An `unlisted import use` advisory is the loader telling you a name resolved through the census -- for a coproduct or record it also pulled, for an alias it did not. And an `effect summary incomplete` error over a callee whose own module is reported unresolved elsewhere in the SAME run is this class, not an effect-modelling gap.",
    "LIVE RECEIPT, Pkg14 (2026-09-22): the row's partial-import sentence, realized. `v2.test.claim.compiler_materialization_witness` carried TWO import lines (std.realization, v2.std.diagnostic) and bare-referenced 27 declarations in four modules it did not import -- `v2.compiler.materialization_carriers`, `v2.compiler.parse`, `extdeps.realization.compile_stage_memo`, `extdeps.realization.parse_table_memo`. Gate one turned the bare channel off for the file, so `claim_batch --print-entry-closure` showed a closure of 74 files with none of the four, every name resolved through the census, and `claim_batch --entry` on the module refused with 30 `effect summary incomplete: <claim> calls <callee>, whose identity is established but names no registry row` errors: the module's claims could not run locally at all, only on the required floor. Two of the 27 callees were `data` declarations (`compile_stage_memo_id`, `parse_table_memo_id`), which sent three lanes after the REFERENCE SHAPE -- every data reference, a name collision with a near-twin fn, `==` with a literal or resolved left operand -- over four probes in `v2.test.provenance.parse_memo_frame_replay`, a ZERO-import module whose closure already held all three of the modules those probes touched, so no probe could reproduce it. A `data` callee is not a defect: `v1.compiler.infer` `analyze_item` gives an initialised data declaration a zero-arg signature and `build_item_info` gives every item a registry row, so it joins whenever its module is realized. REPAIR: the witness imports every name it references (closure 97 modules, all 13 claims PASS locally, 0 effect-summary errors); nothing in the loader or the effect join changed, because the refusal was correct. NO NEW FIXTURE: gate one is already discriminated by `fixtures/bare_reference_channel/imported_record_consumer`. SIZING IS NOT RECORDED because no available reader can produce it: `v1_compiler.cli_run` `bare_reference_channel_readings` answers whether the bare half ran and what it pulled, not which names a gate-one file resolved through the census; a count of files with an import line is an upper bound on exposure, not the class. The count waits on this row's CEILING TRIGGER, the per-file decline carrier.",
    "GATE ONE STAYS, AND WHY: THE DEEPER READING WAS MEASURED AND REFUSED ON COST (2026-09-24, wise-ibex-457, ruled by the parent lane). The proposal above -- pull the declarer of every census-resolved bare name whatever the file's import header -- was built as two claim_batch binaries from main cc90b751bb7 that differ ONLY in removing `source_declares_import_lines` from `v1_compiler.cli_run` `admit_pool_bare_references` and `build_both_closure_edge_index`, and each witness below was run under `claim_batch --entry` with all its claims. Reproduce by rebuilding that pair; the figures are that experiment's and are not a standing measurement. Gate on -> gate off, (entry-closure files; per-shard peak RSS; wall): test.claim.host_standup_spine 114 -> 914 files, peak unreported (the gate-on run refused first) -> 10.7 GiB, 174s -> 337s, every claim refused -> 21/21 PASS; test.claim.host_phase_status 114 -> 917, unreported -> 10.8 GiB, 190s -> 325s, refused -> 30/30 PASS; test.claim.fleet_converge_plan 1727 -> 1862, 14.7 -> 15.3 GiB, 492s -> 547s, verdicts unchanged; test.claim.long.roadmap_page 760 -> 838, 10.2 -> 10.5 GiB; v2.test.claim.provenance.occurrence_file_attribution 206 -> 212, 8.7 -> 8.7 GiB. Whole-pool admission refused nothing new. THE STOP: fleet_converge_plan crosses the 15.0 GiB admitted entry budget that gunbc#11092 measured against, from a baseline already at 14.7. THE MECHANISM, which is why the growth is not the witness's own: removing the gate turns the bare channel on for EVERY partial-import module already in the closure, production modules included, so a witness's closure grows by those modules' own bare pulls, transitively -- 114 -> 914 is mostly passengers. The specimen repaired by imports alone (gunbc#12191) evaluates at 10.4 GiB with the gate kept.",

    "THE RULE THAT REPLACES IT, and the Pkg14 reading it keeps: the import repair above was right, and what was missing was the refusal that makes it mandatory. `v1_compiler.cli_run` `unimported_bare_providers` derives, through this row's own gate-two provider selection (`visit_bare_reference_providers`, no second scanner), every (file, name) whose file declares an import line and whose declarer lies outside that file's import closure. ONE judgment per file, `unimported_bare_provider_file_refusals`, is applied on BOTH routes -- to each touched `.dag` file in the required floor's diff projection and to each entry of a `claim_batch --entry` run -- and refuses an unrostered pair at the file, naming the module to import it from, so the entry route and the floor can no longer answer differently about the same file. The pairs standing when the rule landed are monotone debt at identity grain in `v2.workflow.floor_unimported_bare_provider_debt_roster`, seeded once and never regenerated; `v2.workflow.floor_unimported_bare_provider_debt` `unimported_bare_provider_roster_edit` refuses growth, a drop without a typed retirement, and a retirement for a pair that was never debt, judged against the roster at the diff base; and the floor holds each retirement's cause to its claim. EXECUTED EVIDENCE AND ITS LIMIT, stated at path grain: on the ENTRY route a planted import line on test.claim.machine_word_witness (zero imports, evaluating) refused at the file with one located UnimportedBareProviderUnrostered line per name, and the unedited file evaluates; the roster-edit verdict is exercised by `v2.test.claim.floor_unimported_bare_provider_debt` (a disabled growth arm turns exactly its claim red) and the base-evaluation path by a seed unit test, which is local diligence because no CI step runs `cargo test`. On the REQUIRED FLOOR the discriminating red was executed on the required lane itself: gunbc#12205 carried a planted commit (686c92d8ef) adding a bare, unimported `srv3_install_hang_no_router_lease_ms` to its touched witness file, and required run 35949810788 refused at that file (`phase=unimported-bare-provider-gate touched_dag_files_checked=4 refusals=1`, `REQUIRED-FLOOR REFUSAL cause=UnimportedBareProviderUnrostered file=src/v2/test/claim/floor_unimported_bare_provider_debt_test.dag name=srv3_install_hang_no_router_lease_ms`, adjudicated BLOCKING); the plant was reverted in the next commit, whose run is the positive control. So the gate-one arm is at rung 2, mechanically preventable, on BOTH routes for any touched file: a new unimported bare provider blocks the landing. The rostered debt stays below it by declaration, bounded by the roster's identities. The remaining climb is the CEILING TRIGGER above, the per-file decline carrier that would name the unpulled module at the distant registry join for rostered files too.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
